WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.390
you can experience a cloud just by
walking around San Francisco

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.650
This fog is just a stratocumulus cloud where its
base is at the surface

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.640
but we don't think of it that way

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.780
we think of of fog as this grey kind of haze

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.060
when it's actually just individual cloud droplets

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.880
that are obscuring our view

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.260
I think if people realize they're walking through a cloud

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.640
that's probably the most important cloud type on earth

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.580
they might think that's pretty cool

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.880
clouds are complicated because there's so
many different types

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.760
some are liquid, some are ice, some are both

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.920
some grow all the way from the base of the atmosphere to the top

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.700
and there's seasonal timings to
worry about

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.280
the biggest question that cloud physicists  are investigating  right now  are

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.980
how cloud droplets and aerosol particles interact with each other

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.800
any particle in the atmosphere is considered an aerosol

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.940
you only make a cloud when you have a particle

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.860
for the water vapor to condense upon

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.180
in general, if you think of a cloud over the ocean

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:03.120
the majority of particles that serve as
the center of a cloud drop are usually

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:07.330
this combination of sea salt and some
biologically produced sulfur from algae

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:11.020
If we look over land dust is a huge
component right because there's lots of

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.979
dust over the desert there's lots of
dust over land

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.140
and then for industrial areas particles from say power plants or from industrial processes

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.640
wherever you are regionally the particle
distribution changes

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.200
so places where there's lots of fires our clouds have
smoke centers

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.490
the smoke is actually amazing and complicated

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:32.880
because it does two things

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.729
if it's on top of the cloud like in San Francisco bay

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:40.090
if you look down from space the sunlight's coming straight from above

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.870
the smoke, it would look much darker so it wouldn't  reflect as much like back

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.020
it would actually heat the earth in that sense

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.400
now eventually the smoke can actually
intermingle with the cloud and not only

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:55.240
does that change how the cloud behaves
it gives us a lot of other things to worry about too

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.180
It changes whether or not it rains

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.020
which determines how long the cloud will stay there

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.200
if we start infusing the cloud with more particles

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.290
we can have new particle formation and have lots of smaller ones

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.390
and now our cloud looks brighter because now we've got more droplets to reflect light

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.560
the stratocumulus example  is great where you'll have
actually off the California coast it's

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.690
actually very easy to see with the
ship's going to and from Hawaii

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.740
the ship track streak will be so much brighter and it looks thicker and fluffier

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.800
than the cloud near it

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.400
so when it's above it makes the cloud look darker,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.820
when it's intermingled it makes it look brighter
so it's a really complex system

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.520
because you could have both happening at the same time

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.120
so how much does one cancel
out the other is really complicated

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.140
you can see that added smoke and added
particles

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.269
from us doing something as a species is changing clouds

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:41.089
it's not just there's lightning and
there's some fires

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.180
the amount of clouds that are on the earth determine how much light is bounced back

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:52.340
if we get rid of those huge cloud masses then there won't be as much energy going back to space

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.400
and the overall temperature of the earth would go up

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.660
we have to know why it is doing what it's doing right
now so that we can be better informed

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.040
about how to calculate what's going to
change in the future especially in

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.310
regions that are developing more people
are doing farming

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.160
there's more particles being emitted and then that could exacerbate the problem

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.380
and maybe deplete the cloud and then it gets warmer on top of that

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.980
the future of our work is to really get an understanding
of those interactions

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:20.349
across a range of aerosols and a range of cloud types

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:23.409
clouds are that important to what temperature the earth is

00:03:23.409 --> 00:03:26.669
that we need to know what those changes are

